From e8038fbeb0269ddb7a30d358e190eeba352e5680 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dimitri John Ledkov <dimitri.ledkov@surgut.co.uk>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 15:23:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Build the legacy provider as a small freestanding module

Reduce the legacy provider to a fixed, minimal algorithm set and make it
linkable without libcrypto, so that the resulting legacy.so depends only
on the C library and can be loaded by a libcrypto it was not built
against.  This is the shape the FIPS provider already has, achieved the
same way: the implementations the module needs are compiled a second time
into liblegacy.a.

The algorithms registered are now MD4, MD5 and SHA1 for digests, and RC4,
RC4-40, AES-128-ECB and AES-256-ECB for ciphers.  Everything else is
dropped, including the KDF and key-management operations, which existed
only to serve algorithms that are no longer offered and which pulled in
EVP fetch and ASN.1 support.

MD5, SHA1 and AES were previously reachable only through libcrypto's
exported symbols, so crypto/sha, crypto/aes and crypto/modes now feed
liblegacy.a as crypto/md5 and crypto/md4 already did.  crypto/md4's
condition is relaxed accordingly: the module needs MD4 whether or not
deprecated symbols were configured out.  A short list of utility sources
in crypto/build.info covers what those implementations call, and the AES
and cipher-common source lists are narrowed so that GCM, CCM, XTS and
AES-CBC-HMAC-SHA are left out.

Everything in liblegacy.a is compiled with LEGACY_MODULE defined, and two
code paths are compiled out under it because they would otherwise pull in
most of the library:

  - OSSL_PARAM_get_BN() and OSSL_PARAM_set_BN() are the only users of
    crypto/bn in this module.  Removing them removes BIGNUM support,
    which none of the algorithms above uses.

  - ossl_cipher_tlsunpadblock() and its caller reach tls_pad.c, which
    calls RAND_bytes_ex() on its constant-time failure path.  No
    TLS-capable cipher is registered, so the path is unreachable.

legacyprov.c no longer creates a child OSSL_LIB_CTX either.  All seven
algorithms are self-contained and never fetch from the core, and dropping
it removes the last libcrypto dependency.

Each algorithm is advertised with "fips=yes" alongside "provider=legacy",
so that it remains fetchable when an application sets default properties
of "fips=yes".  None of them is FIPS-approved; each is non-approved but
allowed for a specific, non-security-relevant purpose in the sense of
FIPS 140-3 IG 2.4.A -- parsing or de-obfuscating data in a format that
mandates it, rather than providing confidentiality, authentication or
integrity.  RC4 and RC4-40 decrypt legacy encrypted PDF documents, MD5 is
required by the PDF format itself, SHA1 verifies artifacts in ecosystems
that publish SHA1 checksums, MD4 is required by webpack's content
hashing, and AES-ECB derives the masking value for QUIC header
protection (RFC 9001) and DTLS 1.3 record number encryption (RFC 9147).

README-LEGACY-FREESTANDING.md documents the recommended configure
settings (no-asm and no-secure-memory are needed, no-quic is optional)
and why each is required, two build gotchas, the per-algorithm rationale
for the FIPS property and its limits, verification commands, and how to
run the relevant tests.

Note that "make test" does not work: it builds every test program, and
test/endecode_test and test/evp_extra_test link liblegacy.a alongside
libcrypto.a and now hit multiple-definition errors on the duplicated
sources.  Use "make run_tests TESTS=..." as described in the README.
Recipes covering the removed algorithms fail by design.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
